fcntl keeps a lock command's argument a capability

fcntl read its variadic argument as unsigned long and passed (void *)arg for
F_GETLK, F_SETLK, F_SETLKW and the F_*OWN_EX commands. On capstone64 a long
holds an address, not a capability, so the struct flock pointer reached the
delegated runtime untagged, and its first read of the structure faulted
(cause 24): CPython's test_posix at test_lockf, and SQLite's unix VFS at its
first lock. fcntl reads the argument as syscall_arg_t, as musl's own
syscall() does, which is a long where a pointer is one and a capability
here. The integer commands pass the same value as before.

--- a/src/fcntl/fcntl.c
+++ b/src/fcntl/fcntl.c
@@ -6,12 +6,12 @@
 
 int fcntl(int fd, int cmd, ...)
 {
-	unsigned long arg;
+	syscall_arg_t arg;
 	va_list ap;
 	va_start(ap, cmd);
-	arg = va_arg(ap, unsigned long);
+	arg = va_arg(ap, syscall_arg_t);
 	va_end(ap);
-	if (cmd == F_SETFL) arg |= O_LARGEFILE;
+	if (cmd == F_SETFL) arg = (syscall_arg_t)((unsigned long)arg | O_LARGEFILE);
 	if (cmd == F_SETLKW) return syscall_cp(SYS_fcntl, fd, cmd, (void *)arg);
 	if (cmd == F_GETOWN) {
 		struct f_owner_ex ex;
